Z||000006||theogre||12-24-2000||08:15 PM||||My7, What browser and version is it? How are you connected to the internet?<P>Bill originally wrote that document in word. I let word2k convert it to HTML then did some cleanup. The end result is allot of style attributes that may not be supported in all browsers. This document does some things with the graphic layout that aren't exactly standard.<P>Try clearing your browser cache and reload the page. That sometimes helps. Also the images are a tad large. Give it awhile to load. (I just flushed the cache and it took a good minute at least to load it all.)<P>Without a complete rework of the article and/or graphics I'm not sure how much I can do to fix problems with NS versions. NS has fallen way behind the curve on supporting IETF and W3 standards. The current version has shown itself to be major buggy and I won't install it. I used to like netscape but since AOHell bought it, the product has really gone to peices.<P>Don't get me wrong IE isn't by any means perfect. IE5.0 has some quirks about loading that page too. IE5.5 doesn't seem to have trouble at all. No one still running IE4.x has reported problems yet. (If you have IE5 upgrade to 5.5, it fixes a number of porblems with 5.0. not just loading that page.)<P>Also,,, the site now uses a number of Java Scripts for navigation and other things. Don't count on the animated indicator in the browsers upper left or the status bar to tell you the site is finished loading. If you aren't getting the X mark in the graphic it just may be moving really slow. <p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 12-24-2000).]||||reg||1
